cMahon and Film Australia", "credit_line": "", "headline_credit": "Australia, 1999", "video_links": [], "record_type": "work", "type": "Film", "is_on_display": false, "last_on_display_place": null, "last_on_display_date": null, "is_context_indigenous": false, "material_description": "", "unpublished": false, "external": false, "first_production_date": "1999-01-01T11:00:00+11:00", "commissioned": false, "external_references": [], "brief_description": "<p>Based on photographer William Yang&#8217;s one man stage show, &#8220;Sadness&#8221; chronicles two diverging narratives through the use of slide photography, oral history and stylised recreation. One story follows Yang&#8217;s pilgrimage into the heart of the North Queensland sugar cane fields as he investigates the murder of his uncle Fang Yuen. The other is a series of moving portraits of the many friends and lovers Yang has lost to AIDS. Director Tony Ayres skillfully weaves these two separate stories together creating a powerful testament to family, friends, love and loss. Cinematography by Tristan Milani. Narrated by William Yang.</p>", "constellations_primary": [], "constellations_other": [], "recommendations": [], "title_for_label": "<p>Sadness: a monologue by William Yang</p>", "creator_credit_for_label": "<p>Tony Ayres, Megan McMurchy, Michael McMahon and Film Australia</p>", "headline_credit_for_label": "<p>Australia, 1999</p>", "description": "<p>Based on photographer William Yang&#8217;s one man stage show, &#8220;Sadness&#8221; chronicles two diverging narratives through the use of slide photography, oral history and stylised recreation. One story follows Yang&#8217;s pilgrimage into the heart of the North Queensland sugar cane fields as he investigates the murder of his uncle Fang Yuen. The other is a series of moving portraits of the many friends and lovers Yang has lost to AIDS. Director Tony Ayres skillfully weaves these two separate stories together creating a powerful testament to family, friends, love and loss. Cinematography by Tristan Milani. Narrated by William Yang.</p>", "description_for_label": ["<p>Based on photographer William Yang&#8217;s one man stage show, &#8220;Sadness&#8221; chronicles two diverging narratives through the use of slide photography, oral history and stylised recreation. One story follows Yang&#8217;s pilgrimage into the heart of the North Queensland sugar cane fields as he investigates the murder of his uncle Fang Yuen. The other is a series of moving portraits of the many friends and lovers Yang has lost to AIDS. Director Tony Ayres skillfully weaves these two separate stories together creating a powerful testament to family, friends, love and loss. Cinematography by Tristan Milani. Narrated by William Yang.</p>"], "credit_line_for_label": "", "details": [{"label": "Duration", "display_values": ["00:52:00:00"]}, {"label": "Language", "display_values": ["English"]}, {"label": "Audience classification", "display_values": ["PG"]}, {"label": "Subject categories", "display_values": ["Advertising, Film, Journalism, Mass Media & TV&nbsp;→ Motion pictures - Awards - Australia", "Agriculture, Business, Commerce & Industry&nbsp;→ Sugar workers - Queensland", "Anthropology, Ethnology, Exploration & Travel&nbsp;→ Chinese - Australia", "Anthropology, Ethnology, Exploration & Travel&nbsp;→ Queensland", "Crafts & Visual Arts&nbsp;→ Documentary photography", "Documentary", "Documentary&nbsp;→ Documentary films - Australia", "Economics, Philosophy, Politics, Religion & Sociology&nbsp;→ Assimilation (Sociology)", "Economics, Philosophy, Politics, Religion & Sociology&nbsp;→ Chinese - Australia", "Food, Health, Lifestyle, Medicine, Psychology & Safety&nbsp;→ AIDS (Disease)", "Food, Health, Lifestyle, Medicine, Psychology & Safety&nbsp;→ AIDS (Disease) - Patients - Australia", "History&nbsp;→ Queensland - History", "Literature&nbsp;→ Monologues", "Places&nbsp;→ Queensland", "Short films", "Short films&nbsp;→ Short films - Australia"]}, {"label": "